! Standing on the operator's hand, never the crew's

''A crew that starts building against an unruled fork wastes a week.'' Each entry below carries a VERDICT measured against the tree on 2026-09-14. ''Three stand genuinely OPEN · one stands OPEN against a stale standing ruling · one stands ALREADY RULED with its ledger prose corrected · two read MISFRAMED.'' Build against none of the first four without the operator's word.

!! OPEN — named unruled in a carrier or in source

''① `tend.repair` reaching the herm's `@daemon` signing key.'' <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/lamplighters">> `:369-371` carries the section header `★ OPEN — the voice-capture question, unruled ★` and states it: `tend.repair` scoped as admin over the herm's own island reaches that island's `@daemon` SIGNING KEY. Two readings stand named and ''the operator has ruled neither'' — ISOLATE the signing key from the tending cap's blast radius, or ACCEPT the reach and price the loss of independent herm testimony. The carrier's own `role` line (`:12`) advertises the question. ''Vocabulary trap:'' `tend.repair` names a CAP, never a CLI verb — no `lares tend` verb stands, and `lares vault repair` (`packages/lares-cli/src/commands/vault.ts:162`) reads as a different thing entirely. A sub-clause in the same section (`★ STRUCK — operate ⊥ sign DOES NOT HOLD ★`) kills one premise and proposes a FROST threshold Place-DID, closing on ''the operator rules whether that split holds'' — so the narrowing landed and the ruling did not.

''② Seam (b)'s altitude.'' Seam (b) names ''the ungated slot child'': a slot child edited in the browser rides the native PUT ungated, because `framedRootOf` reads null on a child — <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/syncer-back-parity">> `:58`, `:82`, the lean at `:176`, and the outcome block at `:169` (''(b) STANDS, and now says why'' — the backstop widening REFUTED at its red, `3dfb06170` — closing ''The operator's call''). The altitude question itself: at which altitude does the grade read — ''the child's bytes alone, or the ROOT's composed render where the frame fault comes into being'' (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/child-grade-decision">> `:373`, also `:194`, `:240`). The measurement that forces the question: the paste that opened seam (b) grades `clean` on the child's own bytes and shows as `2 text frames` in `readCarrierShape` over the ROOT's served render, ''so the fault composes at the join and neither fork's altitude reads it'' (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/syncer-seams-roundtable">> `:378`). The decision surface stands BUILT and unruled (`0c911b513`). ''Precondition:'' the operator's word. Nothing else blocks it.

''③ `hearthGatePubKey` consumed as `anchorGateKey`.'' This one lives ONLY in source and one commit message — no `.mem` carrier mentions `hearthGatePubKey` anywhere. The mint: `packages/lararium-node/src/commands/device-admit.ts:36` (the `CarriedAdmitPayload` extension) and `:219` (`hearthGatePubKey: founderVesselKey.toLowerCase()`). The pass-as: `packages/lararium-node/src/commands/init.ts:221` — `anchorGateKey: payload.hearthGatePubKey ?? null`, inside `nexusIdentity({…})`; pinned to disk at `:234` and read back at `:451`. Resolution chain: `packages/lararium-node/src/nexus-standing.ts:63`, whose own `:31` notes it ''does not settle `anchorGateKey`'s OWN PRECEDENCE for a verb with a CLI override''; consumer `packages/lararium-mesh/src/nexus-identity.ts:125,142`. ''The shape of the open loop, verbatim from `device-admit.ts:206-218`:'' the gate key must stay the founder's own VESSEL key, because it carries the anti-relay binding the joinee's V3 proof commits to — the charter island would break that commitment and reads as no fix. But the joinee consumes it as its `anchorGateKey`, resolves `kind: "anchor"`, and seats its inception on a board keyed on the FOUNDER'S VESSEL KEY. ''A founder that has CLIMBED to a charter therefore hands its device an island the founder does not itself stand on'' — founder boots to `epoch0-…`, device reads the vessel key, two DISJOINT persona-KEL boards. And `init.ts:218-220`'s own comment asserts the invariant this breaks (''An admit hands over the hearth's gate key, so the joinee seats its inception on the board its HEARTH stands''), which reads FALSE for a climbed hearth. ''Splitting the DIAL binding from the ISLAND the pin implies reads as a ruling about the wire, never a read to correct'' — `5251f49b2` names it under the banner `THE SECOND-ORDER DEFECT, NAMED AND LEFT STANDING (a ruling is owed, not a read)`.

!! OPEN against a stale standing ruling — read both hands

''④ `bag-residency.ts`'s home.'' The file stands at `packages/lararium-mesh/src/bag-residency.ts` (489 lines, `@lararium/mesh`; imported by `cabal-realm.ts` and `carriage-caps.ts`). <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/api/residency-tiers">> `:96` carries an ''operator ruling of 2026-06-01'' — residency methods MUST move out of the side-file into the causal-island code — immediately followed by `⚠ THE RELOCATION STANDS UNENACTED, and the tree argues a different cure` (re-measured 2026-09-13: no `causal-island.ts` stands). `:98` carries the code's counter-argument in the code's own words (`bag-residency.ts:12-15`, `ONE FILE, ONE QUESTION`); see also `:44`. ''Do NOT read `02d7f24bf` as ruling this.'' That commit rules the ADJACENT question — the word collision, ''two offices, one word, NEITHER RENAMING'' — and says of this item verbatim: the clause ''now reads as a ruling awaiting a hand, beside the counter-argument the code carries in its own words, ''with the fork between them left OPEN''''.

!! ALREADY RULED — the ledger carve-out

''⑤ The crossings-deadlock's permanent shape — closed.'' `0d00d75f5` exempts the ledger path in `tools/hearths-gate.mjs`'s `hearthsOf`, so a hold cannot prevent another hearth from recording a crossing. `tools/hearths-gate-witness.sh` pins the carve-out. The aftermath sweep also corrected the ledger's stale alternatives; no new operator ruling waits here.

!! MISFRAMED — the question does not stand where it reads

''⑥ Break (c) — not a which-key question.'' The breaks enumeration lives at <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/waking-floor">> `:213-247` (`!! ⚠ Six breaks ~ what the proposal got wrong`, (a) through (f)) — note `api/pono`, never `docs/pono`. Break (c), `:231-234`, verbatim: ''(c) THE ANNOUNCEMENT, UNSIGNED, ARMS TWO ATTACKS.'' It becomes a spoofable degradation signal (peers route away from a healthy vessel) — and under the standing brief `shelter against empire` it becomes a TARGETING BEACON, naming which vessel sits unraised, right now, and where. ''The floor's announcement MUST sign''. So the MUST-sign mandate reads as the break's own stated cure, never as an open fork. ''And the which-key half stands answered by standing law:'' `waking-floor.mem:149` rules `SEATED ⊥ RAISED` (a Herm's `personaSlotCeiling === 0` bars a SEATED persona root; a raise seats nothing), and the signer for a faceless vessel reads as its device-minted Place DID per the 2026-09-12 heraldry ruling — <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/heraldry">> `:14`, `:35` (''a Place DID is an owner''). ''What waking-floor DOES leave open'' (`:185`, `:307`): what ''recognition'' means, and the renewal interval. A crew wanting an open floor question takes those two, not (c).

''⑦ The `anchors-h{N}` census — owed by nobody.'' `grep -rn "anchors-h" bags/` returns ZERO hits: no carrier names such a census. Anchors sit outside the key census ''by law, never by omission'' — `packages/lararium-node/src/identity-anchors.ts:27-29` states `UNIFORM KEYING — every index spells anchors-h${N}.json (no founding special-case)` and ''Anchors carry PUBLIC doc-ids, so no seal touches them''; the census itself matches only key-bearing families (`packages/lararium-node/src/key-class.ts:61-62`, ''A card is PUBLIC and never listed''; `vesselKeyCensus` at `:65-92`). Enumerating anchored indices stands RULED and BUILT the explicit way — the written `anchor-roster.json`, ''never a dir-scan'' (`identity-anchors.ts:34-57`, `listAnchoredPersonas` at `:199-209`, contract at `packages/lararium-mesh/src/anchor-store.ts:49-51`). ''And the opposite discipline stands ruled for the SECRET h{N} family'', which is the part that reads as a contradiction and is not: `2fe5e41b7` has `deviceShareCarriers()` SCAN the disk for `recovery-device-share-h<N>.bin`, expressly rejecting a roster — ''the anchor/persona rosters record a different fact and read `[]` when torn; a handle COUNT would skip a gap in the numbering''. Two facts, two enumeration disciplines, both ruled.

